fact WorkItem(id: "WI-763", description: some(value: "Grammar: admit a literal as a named-tuple TYPE component, so a keep-spec (and any denoted-bearing tuple type) is WRITABLE. CONTEXT: WI-732 added Project[T, Keep], where Keep is a named-tuple TYPE whose field name is each result key and whose component is a denoted carrying the source column's name -- Project[T = (name: String, age: Int64), Keep = (person: \"name\", years: \"age\")] reduces to (person: String, years: Int64). The typer SYNTHESIZES that Keep and it reduces correctly, but it CANNOT BE WRITTEN: type-ARGUMENT position admits a literal (_common_type_expr in tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js:156-166, the channel WI-759's FieldOf[Name = \"age\"] uses), while a named-tuple type COMPONENT does not (_tuple_type_arg is choice($._type, $.field_decl), grammar.js:1481). So Project is today reducible-when-synthesized and writable-only-abstractly (over type params), unlike Concat/Without/FieldOf which are fully writable -- the family is inconsistent in surface syntax even though it shares one reduction boundary. CONSEQUENCE beyond ergonomics: project_schema_type's and keep_spec_projections' diagnostics (a Keep naming no column, a non-denoted component) are currently UNREACHABLE, because the only producer is the typer itself and the forward gate shares projection_columns with the reduction -- so they cannot be tested. NOTE the naive fix is wrong: widening field_decl's RHS would let a literal into ENTITY field declarations too (entity person(name: \"foo\")). Wants a component production scoped to tuple TYPES. GOTCHA: run cargo clean -p tree-sitter-anthill after editing grammar.js; watch for GLR ties (see the prec.dynamic comment-tie precedent). ACCEPTANCE: Keep = (person: \"name\") parses in a signature and reduces; the Project reduction's error paths become reachable and get tests; npx tree-sitter test corpus green; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T23:09:13Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-763", author: "user", content: "RENUMBERED WI-761 -> WI-763 on merge with origin/main, same collision as WI-762. Upstream filed a different WI-761 at 16:49Z (CppCodegenError conflates nothing-to-emit with failed-to-emit); it is cited by name in WI-576 feedback, so it keeps the number. NOTE commit 85db6f98 still reads \"WI-761 filed\" for this ticket.", at: "2026-07-18T21:30:25Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-763", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Grammar: new `denoted_field_decl` (`person: \"name\"`) + hidden `_type_literal` shared with `_common_type_expr`; `tuple_type` gains a ONE-component denoted form. DEVIATION from the ticket's stated approach, deliberate: the denoted component rides in the SHARED `_tuple_type_arg`, not a tuple-scoped symbol. Measured why -- `arrow_params` and `tuple_type` share a prefix and are told apart only by the `->` after `)`, so two component symbols make that prefix ambiguous at every `,` (tree-sitter reported exactly this conflict), i.e. a GLR fork on EVERY named arrow param list. The ticket's actual requirement is met: `field_decl` is untouched, so `entity person(name: \"foo\")` stays a parse error. An arrow param with a literal is refused at CONVERSION instead (located message, better than a syntax error). The one-component form uses prec(1) not a declared conflict -- the arrow reading is wrong for every such input, so it is resolved statically with no runtime fork. ACCEPTANCE MET: `Keep = (person: \"name\")` parses and reduces; all three previously-unreachable diagnostics now fire from written source (verified by PRINTING them, not by contains()); 12 new tests; corpus 181/181; workspace 3076 passed / 0 failed. FOUND WHILE REVIEWING (all fixed, see tests): (1) comments are tree-sitter `extras` so they appear among named children -- the new loud arm would have rejected every commented tuple type, and the SIBLING `convert_arrow_type` already had this bug, failing a whole file over a comment in a param list; (2) `arrow_type` was missing from convert_tuple_type's positional list, so `((A) -> B, C)` SILENTLY became a 1-tuple; (3) a written `Keep` with a duplicate result key built a duplicate-keyed schema `(a: String, a: Int64)` with no diagnostic, contradicting the stdlib doc's claim that duplicate result keys are rejected (true only for the dot surface) -- now checked in keep_spec_projections. STILL OPEN, out of scope: a duplicate-key named tuple TYPE is accepted language-wide (`operation f() -> (a: Int64, a: String)` loads clean); rejecting that is a broader change. Also still unwritable: a general one-component named tuple `(a: A)`, for the arrow_params reason above.", at: "2026-07-18T23:09:09Z")
